> Input rounding and atomic finite-state contracts

At sufficiently large offsets, distinct real perturbations can round to the same
double. No streaming recurrence can reconstruct those missing differences. The
final control explicitly shifts[-1,0,1] by10^16; the resulting rounded inputs
coincide, and the same-input reference has zero spread. Relative errors then use
flagged0 placeholders, not an invented claim of recovered unit variance. The
other rows retain the original10000-value base stream under larger offsets. The
final plot displays log10(count+1) so even the last few distinct values remain
visible; the table retains raw counts.

Finite observations are required. Count overflow and nonfinite or
unrepresentable moment states throw before mutation. Safe ordinary double
update/merge order is retained; unsafe intermediates use long double before a
checked finite-double commit. The paired covariance addition validates both
marginal additions and its cross moment on copies, committing all states
together. This guards failures without asserting universal statistical or
numerical accuracy. Zero-spread correlation retains the historical0 convention.

Each streaming addition and merge uses O(1) state/work. Full trace capture first
validates every observation and pre-admits2+N fixed records: initial/final
states and each actual addition retaining its original input. Default1000000,
zero disables; fixed scalar fields are part of their record rather than
individually counted. No partial trace escapes failure, no caller references are
retained, and this is not a byte or peak-RAM quota. The offset study separately
limits the complete number of shifted work values and documents its own
diagnostic methods.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/online_variance_rounding.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto data = dm::algorithms::online_variance_demo_data();
    auto rows =
        dm::algorithms::online_variance_offset_study(data.base, {9, 12, 15, 16});
    auto collapsed =
        dm::algorithms::online_variance_offset_study({-1, 0, 1}, {16});
    rows.push_back(collapsed[0]);
    std::vector<std::string> labels{"base+1e9", "base+1e12", "base+1e15",
                                    "base+1e16", "3 values+1e16"};
    std::vector<double> distinct;
    std::print("| explicit control | actual input count | actual distinct rounded values | actual Welford " "sample variance | wide same-input reference | reference has spread |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows.size(); ++i) {
        auto &r = rows[i];
        distinct.push_back(std::log10(double(r.distinct_values) + 1.));
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {} |\n", labels[i],
                   r.count, r.distinct_values, r.welford,
                   double(r.wide_reference), r.wide_reference_spread);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(distinct, labels, "distinct counts");
    p.title("Rounded inputs: remaining distinct values").x_label(
        "explicit stream/offset control").y_label("log10(actual distinct input count +1)").save_svg("build/online_variance_rounding.svg");
}
```
